# No database file is ever committed. The schema lives in migrations/,
# the data lives in Postgres, and a .db in the repository is neither.
*.db-journal
*.sqlite
corpus.db*
# ...except the shipped archive, which is the one exception and is deliberate.
#
# corpus.db is a build artefact and stays ignored. But the rule above meant a
# deployment made from this repository had NO NCERT in it — eighteen books and
# seven thousand passages missing, retrieval quietly falling back to the
# site's own coding lessons, and every science question answered from the
# model's memory on a product sold as "answers from the syllabus".
#
# Rebuilding on the server is not possible: the builder fetches a few hundred
# PDFs and takes about three hours. So the corpus travels with the code,
# gzipped to 6.7 MB, and is unpacked at boot in 0.04 seconds. Committing a
# binary is a real cost; shipping a product that cannot do the thing it is
# sold for is a larger one.
!corpus.db.gz
*.bak

# Any .env variant, not just the exact name. A credential in
# .env.local or .env.backup is the same credential.
.env*
# The picture archive is a build artefact like corpus.db; its .gz ships.
corpus-pics.db
corpus-pics.db.from
corpus-pics.db.unpacking
!corpus-pics.db.gz
